pg:Layout a ipo:LayoutPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explorer, main area and detail card"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The page puts the chapter explorer at the left, the main area in the centre and a detail card at the right that stays closed until the reader asks for a detail."@en .

pg:Main a ipo:MainContent,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main area"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main area shows the chapter content of the selected menu item, with the widgets of its sections inline."@en .

pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Every Bitcoin block header is exactly 80 bytes: 4 for the version, 32 for the previous block hash, 32 for the merkle root, 4 each for the time, the target and the nonce (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin Core keeps each chain apart in its own subdirectory of the data directory, so that a developer can run mainnet and a test chain on one machine: regtest, testnet3, testnet4 and signet, with the main chain in the ..."@en .

pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The six fields of the header sit in a fixed order in the 80 bytes: version, previous block hash, merkle root, time, bits, nonce (Bitcoin Project, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin Core keeps each chain apart in its own subdirectory of the data directory, so that a developer can run mainnet and a test chain on one machine: regtest, testnet3, testnet4 and signet, with the main chain in the ..."@en .

pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "This subject asks about the public proof-of-work test network: testnet3, which is deprecated, testnet4 that replaces it, the minimum-difficulty rule, the block storms and the two rules of BIP 94 (Jahr, 2024)."@en .

pg:Q1_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "This subject asks how blocks are linked backward and why that makes history hard to change: the previous-hash field, a real example, the effect of tampering, accumulated work, the old idea of linked time-stamps and the picture ..."@en .

