pg:SectionTree a ipo:TreeNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Section tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The section tree lists the sections of the chapter by their three levels, and choosing one opens that single section."@en .

pg:Sidebar a ipo:Sidebar,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter explorer"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The explorer at the left shows the chapter's sections as a tree and keeps pace with the tabs of the main area."@en .

pg:SourceDocument a ipo:SourceDocument,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter 11 Stage 3 document"@en ;
    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 11 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:Q10_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The test chains use other address prefixes than mainnet so that nobody sends real coins to a test address by mistake (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block is a container: a small header of metadata, then a counter that says how many transactions follow, then the transactions themselves, which make up nearly all of the bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The first table of the chapter starts with a 4-byte field called block size, but that field belongs to the way Bitcoin Core stores blocks on disk and not to the block as it travels between nodes or as consensus sees it ..."@en .

pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The block hash is the SHA-256 of the SHA-256 of the 80-byte header, and nothing else is hashed: the transactions are covered only through the merkle root inside the header (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The genesis block is the one block that no node needs to download, because its numbers are written in the program: chainparams.cpp calls CreateGenesisBlock with the time 1231006505, the nonce 2083236893, the bits 0x1d00ffff, ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Each block commits to its parent by carrying the parent's hash in its previous-block field, and that sequence of hashes is the chain that runs back to the genesis block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book's example is a real pair of blocks: a node whose tip is block 277,314 with the hash 00000000000000027e7b... receives a block whose previousblockhash is that value, and so extends the chain to height 277,315 ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A merkle tree is built from the bottom: each transaction is hashed to give a leaf, and every pair of neighbours is joined (their two 32-byte hashes written one after the other, 64 bytes) and hashed twice again to give the ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "When a level has an odd number of hashes, the last one is paired with itself, so that the tree stays balanced; the book shows this for three transactions, where transaction C is duplicated (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "There is not one Bitcoin blockchain but several: the main chain created on 3 January 2009 is called mainnet, and the book names three more that exist for testing, testnet, signet and regtest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_AddressPrefixes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Address prefixes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AddressPrefixes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_AddressPrefixes> ;
    skos:definition "The test chains share the base58 prefixes 111 (addresses starting m or n) and 196 (starting 2) and the bech32 prefix tb, with tb1q for segwit and tb1p for taproot, while regtest uses bcrt1q and bcrt1p, so the book's list of m, n and tb1 leaves out the 2 and the regtest prefix that its own listing uses."@en .

pg:S_AssumedChainSizes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Assumed chain sizes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AssumedChainSizes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_AssumedChainSizes> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's own estimate of the disk space of each chain, kept in chainparams.cpp for the free-space warning, is 856 GB for mainnet, 245 GB for testnet3, 31 GB for testnet4 and 24 GB for the default signet, which makes testnet3 about 8 times the book's 'in excess of 30 GB' and the signet 31 times the 769,525,915 bytes of the book's listing."@en .

pg:S_BackLinks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Back links"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BackLinks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BackLinks> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how blocks are linked backward and why that makes history hard to change: the previous-hash field, a real example, the effect of tampering, accumulated work, the old idea of linked time-stamps and the picture of the ice core (Haber and Stornetta, 1991)."@en .

pg:S_Bip34HeightInCoinbase a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 34 height in the coinbase"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip34HeightInCoinbase" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Bip34HeightInCoinbase> ;
    skos:definition "Since BIP 34 the first item of the coinbase script is the height as a minimally encoded number: the test chain's coinbases start 5100 for height 1 (OP_1), 011100 for 17, 02800000 for 128 and 02000100 for 256, which decode back to the heights, and the book calls the purpose that every coinbase differ."@en .

pg:S_Bip94Rules a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 94 rules"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip94Rules" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Bip94Rules> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 94 changes two rules for testnet4: the retarget starts from the first block of the previous period, and the first block of a period may be no more than 600 seconds earlier than its parent; the code switches this on with enforce_BIP94, which only testnet4 sets, and release 29.0 switched the timewarp rule off for regtest."@en .

pg:S_Block277315Links a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Links of block 277,315"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Block277315Links" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Block277315Links> ;
    skos:definition "Block 277,315 of the real chain has the parent hash 00000000000000027e7b... of block 277,314, version 2, time 1388185038, nonce 4215469401 and merkle root 5e049f40..., exactly the values in the book's listing, with 40 transactions in 43,560 bytes and a difficulty of 1180923195.25803."@en .

pg:S_BlockContainer a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block container"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockContainer" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockContainer> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what a block is as a container: the header, the counter and the transactions, the size field that belongs to the file on disk, the weight limit and the fields that getblock prints today (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_BlockHashIsDoubleSha256 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block hash is double sha256"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockHashIsDoubleSha256" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockHashIsDoubleSha256> ;
    skos:definition "The block hash is the SHA-256 of the SHA-256 of the 80-byte header: the genesis header gives 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f (displayed byte-reversed), whereas a single SHA-256 gives 6dd6c5716cc8e243 and so on, and the header of block 277,315 hashes to the id that mempool.space lists."@en .

pg:S_BlockIdentifiers a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block identifiers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockIdentifiers" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockIdentifiers> ;
    skos:definition "The second section teaches the two names of a block, the hash that is computed from the header and the height that counts the blocks, and why the first is unique and the second is not (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_BlockIsHeaderPlusTransactions a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block is header plus transactions"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockIsHeaderPlusTransactions" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockIsHeaderPlusTransactions> ;
    skos:definition "A block is the 80-byte header, then a counter of the transactions, then the transactions themselves: the genesis block is 80 + 1 + 204 = 285 bytes, and the blocks of the private test chain with 1, 2, 3 and 4 transactions are 250, 472, 694 and 915 bytes, about 222 bytes more for each added transaction."@en .

pg:S_BlockReservedWeight a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block reserved weight"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockReservedWeight" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockReservedWeight> ;
    skos:definition "Block templates of Bitcoin Core stop short of the consensus limit: -blockreservedweight, default 8000, keeps 8,000 weight units for the header, the counter and the coinbase, so the fullest template is 3,992,000, and block 840,000 with 3,993,281 weight units was made by other software."@en .

pg:S_BlockSizeFieldIsFileStorage a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The block size field is file storage"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockSizeFieldIsFileStorage" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockSizeFieldIsFileStorage> ;
    skos:definition "The 4-byte block size of the book's table is not part of the block that nodes exchange: it is the field that Bitcoin Core writes in front of each block in its blk file, after the 4 magic bytes, and on the test chain the genesis block was stored with magic fabfb5da and size 285 (293 bytes on disk in all) once the file was read through the XOR key d462ed9f917fe79d that Bitcoin Core has applied to its block files since release 28.0."@en .

pg:S_BlockStructure a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block structure"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockStructure" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockStructure> ;
    skos:definition "The first section of the chapter opens the block: a header of six fields and a list of transactions, with the sizes, the version flags, the time and the target that the header carries (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_BlockWeightLimit a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block weight limit"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockWeightLimit" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_BlockWeightLimit> ;
    skos:definition "The consensus limit is 4,000,000 weight units, not bytes: block 840,000 held 3,050 transactions with 3,993,281 weight units (99.83 percent of the limit) in only 2,325,617 bytes, 762 bytes per transaction, so a block with witness data can hold less than the 4,000,000 bytes that the book gives as its size."@en .

pg:S_ChainWorkAccumulates a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chain work accumulates"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChainWorkAccumulates" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ChainWorkAccumulates> ;
    skos:definition "Each header adds its work to the total: on the test chain every block at 0x207fffff adds 2, so chain work was 2, 4, 6 and 8 at heights 0 to 3 and 1002 (0x3ea) after 501 blocks, whereas the genesis block of mainnet starts at 0x100010001."@en .

pg:S_ChainsAndAddresses a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chains and addresses"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChainsAndAddresses" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ChainsAndAddresses> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks which chains exist and how they are kept apart: genesis blocks, address prefixes, data directories and the sizes of the chains (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_ChancellorOfTheHeadline a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Chancellor of the headline"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChancellorOfTheHeadline" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ChancellorOfTheHeadline> ;
    skos:definition "The headline of The Times of 3 January 2009 speaks of the Chancellor, and the Chancellor of the Exchequer then in office was Alistair Darling, who served under Gordon Brown from 2007 to 2010, and The Times began in 1785 as The Daily Universal Register."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseMaturity100 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase maturity of 100 blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseMaturity100" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_CoinbaseMaturity100> ;
    skos:definition "The reward of a block can be spent only once the block is 100 blocks deep: with COINBASE_MATURITY = 100 the test chain refused a spend at depth 99 with bad-txns-premature-spend-of-coinbase and allowed it at depth 100."@en .

pg:S_Cve20122459Trees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "CVE-2012-2459 trees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Cve20122459Trees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Cve20122459Trees> ;
    skos:definition "The lists of six and of eight transaction ids [1,2,3,4,5,6] and [1,2,3,4,5,6,5,6] have the same merkle root, and so do [1,2,3] and [1,2,3,3], but [1,2,3,4] and [1,2,3,4,3,4] do not: the duplication of an odd last hash can be forged."@en .

pg:S_DataDirectories a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Data directories"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DataDirectories" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_DataDirectories> ;
    skos:definition "Each chain keeps its data in its own subdirectory, regtest, testnet3, testnet4 and signet, and uses its own port: 8333, 18333, 48333, 38333 and 18444."@en .

pg:S_DevelopmentPipeline a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Development pipeline"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DevelopmentPipeline" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_DevelopmentPipeline> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how the test chains are used together: from regtest to signet or testnet and then to mainnet (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FieldOrderAndEndianness a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Field order and endianness"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FieldOrderAndEndianness" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_FieldOrderAndEndianness> ;
    skos:definition "The six fields in the order of the book's table, with the integers written least significant byte first and the two hashes written in the reverse of the order in which they are displayed, give back exactly the 80 bytes of block 277,315: version 02000000, then the parent hash ending a1632249 in display order, and bits 1903a30c."@en .

pg:S_ForkResolvedByMoreWork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fork resolved by more work"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ForkResolvedByMoreWork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ForkResolvedByMoreWork> ;
    skos:definition "A third block on top of the first child made height 512 the tip, the first child's branch active and the second child a valid-fork branch at height 511 of length 1, so the fork was resolved without anyone deleting a block."@en .

pg:S_ForksAndReorgs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Forks and reorganizations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ForksAndReorgs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ForksAndReorgs> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what happens when two blocks compete: the fork, the switch to the heavier branch and the fall of the odds of a reorganization with depth (Nakamoto, 2008)."@en .

pg:S_FourLeafTree a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tree of four leaves"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FourLeafTree" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_FourLeafTree> ;
    skos:definition "A block with four transactions has a tree of 4, 2 and 1 hashes: H_AB = SHA256(SHA256(H_A || H_B)) and H_CD likewise, and the double hash of H_AB || H_CD equalled the merkle root in the header of the test chain's block."@en .

pg:S_GenesisBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisBlock> ;
    skos:definition "The third section is about the first block, which is written into the program, carries the headline of The Times of 3 January 2009 and can be rebuilt from a few numbers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_GenesisCoinbaseMessage a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis coinbase message"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisCoinbaseMessage" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisCoinbaseMessage> ;
    skos:definition "The coinbase script of the genesis block holds the 69-character text 'The Times 03/Jan/2009 Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks' after a push of the number 486604799 and a push of the number 4, which proves that the block was not made before that day."@en .

pg:S_GenesisCoinbaseUnspendable a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis coinbase unspendable"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisCoinbaseUnspendable" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisCoinbaseUnspendable> ;
    skos:definition "The reward of the genesis block cannot be spent: on the test chain getrawtransaction answers that the genesis coinbase is not an ordinary transaction, gettxout finds no output, and the UTXO set at height 0 holds 0 outputs, a quirk that the Bitcoin Wiki says is not known to be intentional."@en .

pg:S_GenesisConstants a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis constants"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisConstants" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisConstants> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what the genesis block is made of: the constants in chainparams.cpp, the message, the listing in the book and the transaction shared by most chains (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_GenesisHardcoded a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis hardcoded"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisHardcoded" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisHardcoded> ;
    skos:definition "A node starts with the genesis block built into its code: a Bitcoin Core 31.1 node on the test chain with no peers and no data reported height 0, 0 headers and 293 bytes on disk, and its genesis hash equalled the one rebuilt from chainparams.cpp."@en .

pg:S_GenesisHashRecomputed a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis hash recomputed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisHashRecomputed" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisHashRecomputed> ;
    skos:definition "The genesis block can be rebuilt from the numbers in chainparams.cpp, the time 1231006505, the nonce 2083236893, the bits 0x1d00ffff and the message: the 204-byte coinbase gives the transaction id 4a5e1e4b... and the 80-byte header hashes to 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f."@en .

pg:S_GenesisListingMatchesBook a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis listing matches book"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisListingMatchesBook" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisListingMatchesBook> ;
    skos:definition "The book's getblock listing of the genesis block agrees with the data of mempool.space in the size 285, the weight 1140, the nonce 2083236893, the bits 1d00ffff (0x1d00ffff), the time 1231006505 and the one transaction, and chain work 0x100010001 is the work of one block at difficulty 1."@en .

pg:S_GenesisQuirks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Genesis quirks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisQuirks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GenesisQuirks> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what is odd about the genesis block: the reward that cannot be spent, and the headline that dates it to the term of Chancellor Alistair Darling (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_GetblockOutputToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The getblock output today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GetblockOutputToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GetblockOutputToday> ;
    skos:definition "The getblock listing of Bitcoin Core 31.1 has two fields that the book's listing lacks: target, added by release 29.0, and the coinbase_tx object (version, locktime, sequence, coinbase, witness) that release 31.0 added; the test chain's genesis block shows both, and the book's nextblockhash is absent only because the genesis block was the tip."@en .

pg:S_GlacierCoreAnalogy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Glacier core analogy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GlacierCoreAnalogy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_GlacierCoreAnalogy> ;
    skos:definition "The book's picture of layers of ice: six blocks are about 60 minutes of topsoil and 100 blocks about 16.7 hours, while ice cores, formed from annual layers of snow, reach back nearly 800,000 years at Dome C, and cores of 2016 from the Allan Hills held ice up to 2.7 million years old."@en .

pg:S_HashChainedTimestamps a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash-chained timestamps"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashChainedTimestamps" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HashChainedTimestamps> ;
    skos:definition "The idea of binding each item to the one before it by a hash is older than Bitcoin: Haber and Stornetta described linked time-stamping of digital documents in 1991, and Nakamoto's paper cites them for the timestamp server."@en .

pg:S_HashIdentity a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash identity"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashIdentity" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HashIdentity> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what the block hash is: a double SHA-256 of the header, which is not stored inside the block and whose zeros are a consequence of the target (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_HashLeadingZeros a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash leading zeros"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashLeadingZeros" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HashLeadingZeros> ;
    skos:definition "The long run of zeros in a block hash is a consequence of the target and is not stored: the genesis hash starts with 10 zero hexadecimal digits, block 277,315 with 15 and block 840,000 with 19, each hash is no larger than its target, and the test chain's genesis hash, at the easiest possible target, starts with only 0."@en .

pg:S_HashNotStoredInBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash not stored in block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashNotStoredInBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HashNotStoredInBlock> ;
    skos:definition "A block does not contain its own hash: the 285 bytes of the test chain's genesis block hold neither 0f9188f1... nor its reverse, but the header of block 481 holds the reversed hash of block 480, so the hash is computed on receipt and then stored by the child."@en .

pg:S_HashTreeHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash tree history"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashTreeHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HashTreeHistory> ;
    skos:definition "The hash tree is named after Ralph Merkle, who patented it in 1979 (US patent 4309569 of 1982) and described it for public-key protocols in a paper of 1980."@en .

pg:S_HeaderFields a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Header fields"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderFields" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HeaderFields> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what is in the 80 bytes: the version bits, the field order and byte order, the timestamp and the median of eleven, the compact target and the nonce that is too small for the work (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_HeaderIs80Bytes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The header is 80 bytes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderIs80Bytes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HeaderIs80Bytes> ;
    skos:definition "Every header, of the genesis block, of block 277,315 and of block 120 of the test chain, is exactly 80 bytes, and since MAX_BLOCK_SERIALIZED_SIZE is 4,000,000 a full block can be 4,000,000 / 80 = 50,000 times the header, the book's 'almost 50,000 times'."@en .

pg:S_HeightAndMaturity a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Height and maturity"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeightAndMaturity" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HeightAndMaturity> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what the height is: a count from the genesis block that can be shared by competing blocks, that BIP 34 writes into the coinbase and that decides when a reward may be spent (Andresen, 2012)."@en .

pg:S_HeightFromGenesis a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Height from genesis"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeightFromGenesis" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HeightFromGenesis> ;
    skos:definition "The height counts blocks from the genesis block at height 0: on 2026-10-15 the tip of mainnet was at height 971,537, so 971,538 blocks, 18.5 years at ten minutes each, against the 800,000 that the book reports for mid 2023, and block 840,000 was found on 2024-04-20."@en .

pg:S_HeightIsNotUnique a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Height is not unique"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeightIsNotUnique" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_HeightIsNotUnique> ;
    skos:definition "A hash names one block, a height does not: on the test chain two children of the same parent were both at height 511 with different hashes, and the book's tip says so in a box."@en .

pg:S_LinkingBlocks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Linking blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LinkingBlocks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_LinkingBlocks> ;
    skos:definition "The fourth section is about the chain itself: how each block commits to its parent, why changing an old block means mining all that follow it, what a fork is and how the odds of a reorganization fall with depth (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MedianTimePast a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Median time past"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MedianTimePast" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MedianTimePast> ;
    skos:definition "A block's timestamp must be later than the median of the previous 11 timestamps: on the test chain this median of blocks 111 to 121 was 1792052132, and block 277,315 of the real chain has a timestamp 1,651 seconds later than its median time past of 1388183387, which BIP 113 uses as the clock for lock times."@en .

pg:S_MerklePathOf16 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkle path of 16 transactions"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerklePathOf16" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MerklePathOf16> ;
    skos:definition "For the 11th of 16 transactions (index 10, the K of the book's figure) the path has 4 hashes, 128 bytes, with the siblings on the right, left, right and left, and following it gave the merkle root of the test chain's 16-transaction block."@en .

pg:S_MerkleProofDirection a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkle proof direction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerkleProofDirection" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MerkleProofDirection> ;
    skos:definition "The order of every pair matters: following the path with the right sides swapped did not give the root, and the sides are the bits of the transaction's position, 1010 read from the lowest bit for index 10."@en .

pg:S_MerkleProofs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkle proofs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerkleProofs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MerkleProofs> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how inclusion is proved with a short path: the path of the 11th of 16 transactions, its logarithmic length, the size compared with a block, the merkleblock message and the importance of left and right (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MerkleTrees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkle trees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerkleTrees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MerkleTrees> ;
    skos:definition "The fifth section teaches the merkle tree that summarizes the transactions of a block in 32 bytes: how it is built, its two known weaknesses and how a short path proves that one transaction is in a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MerkleblockForLightClients a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkleblock for light clients"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerkleblockForLightClients" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MerkleblockForLightClients> ;
    skos:definition "The merkleblock that Bitcoin Core made for one transaction of the test chain's 16-transaction block was 248 bytes (header, count 16, 5 hashes, flags) against the block's 3,580 bytes, and verifytxoutproof returned the transaction; for a block of 4 transactions it was 183 bytes."@en .

pg:S_MutationDefenseInSource a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mutation defence in the source"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MutationDefenseInSource" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_MutationDefenseInSource> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's merkle.cpp warns of CVE-2012-2459 and reports a mutated block, validation.cpp rejects it as bad-txns-duplicate (the run showed the genuine block accepted afterwards), and Optech dates the report to 2012 and a variation to version 0.13.0 of 2016."@en .

pg:S_NoDefaultWallet a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "No default wallet"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NoDefaultWallet" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_NoDefaultWallet> ;
    skos:definition "The book's commands do not run on Bitcoin Core 31.1: createwallet with the empty name '' is refused with 'Wallet name cannot be empty' (exit code 8), a fresh node has no wallet, and getnewaddress answers that no wallet is loaded."@en .

pg:S_NodeRejectsMutatedBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Node rejects mutated block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NodeRejectsMutatedBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_NodeRejectsMutatedBlock> ;
    skos:definition "A block of 3 transactions was changed into one of 4 by repeating the last transaction, with the same header and the same hash: submitblock answered bad-txns-duplicate, and the original block was then accepted and became the tip, so the invalid form did not block the valid one."@en .

pg:S_NonceAndExtraNonce a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Nonce and extra nonce"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceAndExtraNonce" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_NonceAndExtraNonce> ;
    skos:definition "The 4-byte nonce offers only 2^32 = 4,294,967,296 values, yet at the difficulty of block 277,315 a block takes about 5 x 10^18 hashes, which is 1,180,941,215 times the nonce space, so miners must change something else as well (the extra nonce in the coinbase, spare version bits, the timestamp); the test chain's blocks needed nonce 1."@en .

pg:S_OddLeafDuplicated a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Odd leaf duplicated"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OddLeafDuplicated" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_OddLeafDuplicated> ;
    skos:definition "With three transactions the third is paired with itself: the tree has 3, 2 and 1 hashes, the root h(h(A||B) || h(C||C)) equalled the header's merkle root, and this rule reproduced the root of all eight test blocks of 1 to 8 transactions."@en .

pg:S_PathLengthLogarithm a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Path length logarithm"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PathLengthLogarithm" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_PathLengthLogarithm> ;
    skos:definition "A path for 1,024 transactions has 10 hashes (320 bytes), for 4,096 has 12 (384 bytes) and for 16,000 has 14 (448 bytes), and block 277,315 with 40 transactions needs 6."@en .

pg:S_PipelineOrder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pipeline order"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PipelineOrder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_PipelineOrder> ;
    skos:definition "The three stages differ in what the code gives them: regtest has one dummy seed and no public peers, signet 2 seeds and a signed block rule, testnet3 5 seeds and the minimum-difficulty rule, mainnet 8 seeds, the order the book recommends."@en .

pg:S_PreviousHashPointsToParent a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Previous hash points to parent"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PreviousHashPointsToParent" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_PreviousHashPointsToParent> ;
    skos:definition "In ten consecutive headers of the test chain, heights 480 to 489, every header's previous-hash field equalled the recomputed hash of the header before: 9 of 9 links."@en .

pg:S_ProofSizeVsBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Proof size vs block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProofSizeVsBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ProofSizeVsBlock> ;
    skos:definition "A proof of 80 + 448 = 528 bytes is 7,576 times smaller than a block of 4,000,000 bytes, and with a typical 250-byte transaction added 5,141 times smaller, so the book's 'almost 10,000 times' would need a proof of 400 bytes."@en .

pg:S_RealBlockRoot a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Real block root"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RealBlockRoot" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_RealBlockRoot> ;
    skos:definition "From the 40 transaction ids of block 277,315, 257e7497... to 05cfd38f..., the code reproduces the merkle root 5e049f40... that the block header and the book give."@en .

pg:S_Regtest a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Regtest" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Regtest> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks about the private test chain that the reader can run: its start state, the halving every 150 blocks, the balance after 500 blocks and the wallet command of the book that no longer works (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_RegtestBalanceAfter500 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest balance after 500 blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RegtestBalanceAfter500" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_RegtestBalanceAfter500> ;
    skos:definition "After 500 blocks the wallet showed 12462.5 coins as spendable and 931.25 as immature, which the sums of the halving rewards of blocks 1 to 400 and 401 to 500 give, as in the book; mining the 500 blocks took 2.27 seconds in this run."@en .

pg:S_RegtestHalvesEvery150 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest halves every 150 blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RegtestHalvesEvery150" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_RegtestHalvesEvery150> ;
    skos:definition "The reward on regtest halves every 150 blocks instead of every 210,000: 50 coins for heights 1 to 149, 25 from 150, 12.5 from 300 and 6.25 from 450."@en .

pg:S_RegtestStartState a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest start state"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RegtestStartState" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_RegtestStartState> ;
    skos:definition "A fresh regtest node reports exactly the book's values: chain regtest, 0 blocks and 0 headers, difficulty 4.656542373906925e-10, median time 1296688602 and best block 0f9188f13cb7b2c71f2a335e3a4fc328bf5beb436012afca590b1a11466e2206."@en .

pg:S_ReorgProbabilityFalls a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Reorganization probability falls"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReorgProbabilityFalls" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_ReorgProbabilityFalls> ;
    skos:definition "Nakamoto's calculation gives the chance that an attacker with 10 percent of the hash rate ever catches up as 1.0, 0.2045873, 0.0509779, 0.0002428 and 0.0000012 when the block is 0, 1, 2, 6 and 10 deep, and 0.1321 for a share of 30 percent at 6 blocks, the numbers behind the book's six topsoil blocks."@en .

pg:S_RootIsAlways32Bytes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Root is always32 bytes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RootIsAlways32Bytes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_RootIsAlways32Bytes> ;
    skos:definition "The root is 32 bytes whether the block has 1, 2, 8 or 16 transactions or the 3,050 of block 840,000, and with one transaction the root is that transaction's id, as in the genesis block."@en .

pg:S_SeveralChainsExist a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Several chains exist"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SeveralChainsExist" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SeveralChainsExist> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 knows five chains, main, test, testnet4, signet and regtest, and the genesis hashes that the nodes reported for testnet3, testnet4, signet and regtest equal those rebuilt from chainparams.cpp."@en .

pg:S_SharedGenesisTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Shared genesis transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SharedGenesisTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SharedGenesisTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "Mainnet, testnet3, signet and regtest all start from the same 204-byte genesis transaction, id 4a5e1e4b..., and differ only in header (time, nonce, bits), whereas testnet4 has its own coinbase of 180 bytes with id 7aa0a7ae... that carries a different message."@en .

pg:S_Signet a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signet"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Signet" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Signet> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks about the signed test network: the challenge script, the message start derived from it, the shared genesis block and the growth of the default signet (Alm and Towns, 2019)."@en .

pg:S_SignetChallengeIsMultisig a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signet challenge is a multisig"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignetChallengeIsMultisig" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SignetChallengeIsMultisig> ;
    skos:definition "The default signet's challenge is a 71-byte script, OP_1 followed by two 33-byte keys and OP_2 OP_CHECKMULTISIG, so a block needs the signature of one of the two operators, while the custom signet of the run used the one-byte challenge 51 (OP_1), which anyone can satisfy."@en .

pg:S_SignetGenesisShared a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signet genesis shared"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignetGenesisShared" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SignetGenesisShared> ;
    skos:definition "The default signet and the custom signet with the challenge 51 have the same genesis block 000000088198..., time 2020-09-01 00:00:00 UTC and bits 1e0377ae, because the genesis does not contain the challenge."@en .

pg:S_SignetMagicFromChallenge a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signet message start from the challenge"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignetMagicFromChallenge" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SignetMagicFromChallenge> ;
    skos:definition "The four message-start bytes of a signet are the first four bytes of the double SHA-256 of its challenge: 0a03cf40 for the default signet, 54d26fbd for the challenge 51, and 7ec653a5 for the example of BIP 325, so two signets do not talk to each other by mistake."@en .

pg:S_SignetSizeToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signet size today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignetSizeToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SignetSizeToday> ;
    skos:definition "The default signet stood on 2026-10-15 at height 326,440, 2.27 times the 143,619 blocks of the book's listing, near the 321,840 that ten-minute blocks since 2020-09-01 would give, and the book's own figure was about 150,000."@en .

pg:S_SixtyFourByteWeakness a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The 64-byte weakness"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SixtyFourByteWeakness" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_SixtyFourByteWeakness> ;
    skos:definition "An inner node is the hash of 64 bytes, two hashes joined, and a transaction can be exactly 64 bytes without witness data (the smallest is 60), so it can be mistaken for an inner node, the weakness of CVE-2017-12842 that BIP 54 closes by making such transactions invalid."@en .

pg:S_TamperingBreaksDescendants a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tampering breaks descendants"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TamperingBreaksDescendants" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TamperingBreaksDescendants> ;
    skos:definition "Changing one bit of the merkle root in the header of block 483 of the test chain changed its own hash, so the previous-hash link of block 484 no longer matched, and every one of the 7 blocks from 483 to 489 would have to be mined again."@en .

pg:S_TargetCompactEncoding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Target compact encoding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TargetCompactEncoding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TargetCompactEncoding> ;
    skos:definition "The 4-byte target field packs a 256-bit number as one byte of exponent and three of mantissa: 0x1d00ffff is the target 0xffff followed by 26 zero bytes (difficulty 1, the genesis block and chain work 0x100010001), the field 0x1903a30c of block 277,315 gives a difficulty of 1,180,923,195.258, which is the value in the book's listing, and 0x207fffff on the test chain gives the target 7fffff followed by zeros."@en .

pg:S_TestBlockchains a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Test blockchains"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TestBlockchains" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TestBlockchains> ;
    skos:definition "The sixth section is about the chains on which Bitcoin software can be tried without risk: testnet, signet and regtest, with their genesis blocks, addresses, difficulty rules and sizes, and the pipeline from one to the next (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_Testnet a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Testnet"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Testnet" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Testnet> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks about the public proof-of-work test network: testnet3, which is deprecated, testnet4 that replaces it, the minimum-difficulty rule, the block storms and the two rules of BIP 94 (Jahr, 2024)."@en .

pg:S_Testnet3StillThere a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Testnet3 is still there"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Testnet3StillThere" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Testnet3StillThere> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 still starts testnet3 with -testnet (chain name test, directory testnet3), though the help text says the support is deprecated and will be removed, and the chain created on 2011-02-02 stood on 2026-10-15 at height 5,189,394."@en .

pg:S_Testnet4Replaces a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Testnet4 replaces testnet3"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Testnet4Replaces" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_Testnet4Replaces> ;
    skos:definition "Testnet4 begins with a genesis block that quotes the Bitcoin block hash of 3 May 2024, hash 00000000da84f2ba..., time 2024-05-03 23:11:00 UTC, added to Bitcoin Core in release 28.0 by BIP 94, and it stood on 2026-10-15 at height 156,618."@en .

pg:S_TestnetBlockStorms a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Testnet block storms"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TestnetBlockStorms" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TestnetBlockStorms> ;
    skos:definition "Testnet3 has 5,189,394 blocks, 6.3 times the 825,696 that ten-minute blocks would have given since 2011-02-02, and more than 5 times mainnet's 971,537, because the minimum-difficulty rule lets a miner mine in bursts."@en .

pg:S_TestnetMinimumDifficulty a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Testnet minimum difficulty"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TestnetMinimumDifficulty" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TestnetMinimumDifficulty> ;
    skos:definition "The code lets testnet3, testnet4 and regtest (not mainnet, not signet) mine a block at the minimum difficulty when its time is more than 2 x 10 = 20 minutes after the previous block; the book's 'half that of Bitcoin' does not show in the code, in which mainnet and testnet3 have the same limit 00000000ffff....."@en .

pg:S_TimestampField a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Timestamp field"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TimestampField" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TimestampField> ;
    skos:definition "The timestamp is a 4-byte count of seconds since 1970-01-01: the genesis block says 2009-01-03 18:15:05 UTC and block 840,000 says 2024-04-20 00:09:27 UTC, a node refuses a block more than 2 x 60 x 60 = 7,200 seconds in the future, and an unsigned 4-byte counter runs out on 2106-02-07 at 06:28:15 UTC."@en .

pg:S_TransactionCounter a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transaction counter"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransactionCounter" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TransactionCounter> ;
    skos:definition "The transaction counter is a compactSize integer of 1 byte up to 252, 3 bytes for 253 to 65,535 and 5 bytes from 65,536, and a block cannot hold more than 4,000,000 / 240 = 16,666 transactions because the smallest transaction weighs 240 weight units, so the book's 1 to 3 bytes are enough."@en .

pg:S_TreeConstruction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tree construction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TreeConstruction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TreeConstruction> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a merkle tree is built: pairs, the duplicated odd hash, a 32-byte root whatever the size, the depth, a real block and the history of the idea (Merkle, 1980)."@en .

pg:S_TreeDepth a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tree depth"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TreeDepth" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TreeDepth> ;
    skos:definition "The depth of the tree is the ceiling of log2 of the number of leaves: 0 for 1 transaction, 2 for 4, 4 for 16, 6 for the 40 transactions of block 277,315, 12 for the 3,050 of block 840,000 and 14 for 16,000."@en .

pg:S_TreeFlaws a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tree flaws"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TreeFlaws" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TreeFlaws> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks where the construction is weak: the duplicated hash that lets two lists have one root, the defence in the code, the run that shows it, and the 64-byte transaction that looks like an inner node (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_TwoChildrenOneParent a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Two children one parent"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TwoChildrenOneParent" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_TwoChildrenOneParent> ;
    skos:definition "A fork on the test chain: two different blocks with the same parent hash and the same height 511, one active and one a valid-fork branch of length 1."@en .

pg:S_VersionBitsField a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Version bits field"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VersionBitsField" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11#S_VersionBitsField> ;
    skos:definition "The version field is no longer a number but a set of flags: the test chain's blocks carry 0x20000000, the top three bits 001 that BIP 9 requires, while block 840,000 carries 0x2a5fe000, whose bits under the mask 0x1fffe000 (0x0a5fe000) are the 16 bits that the draft BIP 320 reserves for miners to use as extra nonce space."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compute a block's name as the double SHA-256 of its header, and tell a block's hash, its height and its depth apart, including the coinbase maturity of 100 blocks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compute a block's name as the double SHA-256 of its header, and tell a block's hash, its height and its depth apart, including the coinbase maturity of 100 blocks."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Recompute the genesis block's hash from its constants and explain what is special about the first block, its message and its coinbase output"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can recompute the genesis block's hash from its constants and explain what is special about the first block, its message and its coinbase output."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Describe what a block holds (a header of six fields in 80 bytes, a transaction count and the transactions), and why the block size field is file storage and the limit is weight"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can describe what a block holds (a header of six fields in 80 bytes, a transaction count and the transactions), and why the block size field is file storage and the limit is weight."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how back-links chain blocks, why changing an old block means mining it and every later block again, and how a fork is settled by more work"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how back-links chain blocks, why changing an old block means mining it and every later block again, and how a fork is settled by more work."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Build a merkle root and a merkle path by hand, and judge the two weaknesses of the tree (CVE-2012-2459 duplicates and 64-byte confusion) and how a node defends against them"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can build a merkle root and a merkle path by hand, and judge the two weaknesses of the tree (CVE-2012-2459 duplicates and 64-byte confusion) and how a node defends against them."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compare mainnet, testnet3 and testnet4, signet and regtest by purpose and rules, and choose the right test chain for a development step"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch11/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compare mainnet, testnet3 and testnet4, signet and regtest by purpose and rules, and choose the right test chain for a development step."@en .

